d), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \n- You currently hold **10 of Clubs** and **8 of Spades**.  \n- The board is: **7♦, 3♦, 5♠, 7♥, 4♠** (River complete).  \n- This gives you the following possible hand:  \n  - Your best 5-card hand: **7♦, 7♥, 5♠, 4♠, 3♦** → **Pair of 7s**, with 5-high straight draw potential.  \n  - However, your hole cards (10♣, 8♠) do not improve the board. You don’t have a pair yourself, nor do you have a straight or flush.  \n  - The best hand you can make is **a pair of 7s** (using both board 7s), with a kicker of 5, 4, and 3 — but your **10 and 8 are not playing**.  \n  - You have **no pair with your hole cards**, no flush (only two diamonds, you have none), and the straight from 3-4-5-6-7 would require a 6 — you have a 10 and 8, so even 8-7-5-4-3 isn\'t a straight (gaps exist).  \n  - So your hand is **just a pair of 7s**, using the board. Many hands beat this: any 7-x where x > 5, any two pair, trips, straights, flushes, etc.\n\n- Opponent has checked through all rounds. That suggests weakness, but they called pre-flop and never folded, so they likely have something.\n\n- However, **you have no showdown value** — unless opponent has only high cards lower than 7, which is unlikely. Even a single 8, 9, J, etc., paired with a 7 would beat your kicker structure.\n\n- Given that it’s the river, and **both players have checked through every round**, including now, checking will lead to a showdown where you likely lose.\n\n- Possible bluffs? Unlikely to succeed here because:\n  - Board is low and somewhat coordinated.\n  - A raise now looks suspicious after passive play throughout.\n  - Opponent may have weak pairs like 6-6, 5-5, or even bottom two pair and call anyway.\n  - But if they have anything decent (like a 7, a straight, or a set), they’ll call or re-raise.\n\n- Going all-in (-2) risks 19,700 chips on a bluff that probably fails.\n- Folding (-1) forfeits any chance, but saves chips.\n- Checking (0) shows down a weak pair that likely loses.\n\nBut note: **You are in the lead overall (19,700 current chips)**, and this is **hand 18 of 20**. With only 2 hands left, chip preservation becomes more important.\n\nHowever, since each hand starts fresh with 20,000 chips, **your current chip stack doesn’t carry over** — only the cumulative win/loss matters for final outcome. But since the cumulative is fixed until now, and you\'re told “if you have more chips than your opponent by the last hand”, it implies we compare **chip gains** across the 20 hands.\n\nWait: Clarification from prompt: *"If you have more chips than your opponent by the last hand of the game, you win the game."*  \nAnd: *"Everyone starts with 20,000 chips each hand"*, and *"you have 19,700"* → meaning **in this hand**, you’ve lost 300 so far.\n\nBut the *cumulative* win/loss is 19,350 — unclear unit, but likely total net gain/loss across hands.\n\nBut crucially: **each hand is independent in starting stack**, so surviving with more chips at end of this hand contributes to your cumulative edge.\n\nActually, rereading: "you have 19700" → current hand\'s stack. You started with 20,000, now have 19,700 → you\'ve **net lost 300 chips this hand so far** due to blinds/betting.\n\nBut the betting history says:\n- Round 0 (pre-flop): you acted 0 (call/check), opponent raised 200, then you called (action 0 again).\nSo likely: BB is 100? Opponent raised to 200 total (i.e., +200), so you called 200 → you\'ve invested 200.\nThen on flop and turn, both checked → no further bets.\nNow on river, no new bet yet — so pot is small.\n\nSo you still have **19,700 chips left** — you can bet up to that.\n\nBut your hand is very weak: **pair of 7s, no kicker help from your cards**.\n\nOpponent could easily have:\n- 6-8 → straight (3-4-5-6-7)\n- 6-6 → set\n- 8-8 → set\n- A-7 → two pair or better\n- 5-5 → full house\n- Or even just 9-7 → better kicker\n\nYour bes