acing you.\n\nSimilarly, you can’t call.\n\nSo **only valid actions when no bet is facing you are: check (0) or bet (>0)**.\n\nFold (-1) and all-in (-2) are only valid if there is a bet to respond to.\n\nBut the list says: "Your valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100...]"\n\nBut logically, **Fold and All-in should not be valid when it\'s your turn to act first in a round with no prior bet**.\n\nHowever, the system lists them, so perhaps they are always technically allowed, but **folding when not facing a bet is equivalent to surrendering** — which is poor.\n\nSimilarly, going all-in without a bet is a bet of your entire stack.\n\nSo technically, you can:\n\n- Check (0): show weakness, see what opponent does.\n- Bet 100 or more: represent strength.\n- All-in (-2): same as betting 19,700.\n- Fold (-1): give up now — extremely weak, wastes chips already invested.\n\nFolding now would lose 200+ chips for nothing — worse than checking.\n\nAll-in is overkill — betting 19,700 as a bluff is terrible.\n\nSmall bet (100) is a **probe bet** — testing if opponent has anything.\n\nBut given passive history, a small bet might get folds from hands like A-high, K-high, or weak pairs that missed.\n\nBut many players call with any pair.\n\nGiven that you are near the end of the game and in the lead, **minimizing risk** is wise.\n\nBut cumulative chips matter — gaining more in this hand increases your final standing.\n\nStill, losing 19,700 on a bluff is catastrophic.\n\nBest balanced approach: **Check (0)**.\n\nReason: \n- You have no strong hand and have shown weakness throughout.\n- Checking maintains consistency and avoids inflating the pot with a weak hand.\n- It lets the opponent reveal their intent: if they bet, they likely have something; if they check, you go to showdown cheaply.\n- A small bet (100) could be a reasonable bluff, but given your passive line, it lacks credibility and may only get calls from better hands.\n- Going all-in or folding would be extreme and unjustified.\n\nAnswer: 0\n\nFinal Decision:\nEvaluation: Checking preserves chips and avoids escalating with a weak hand. Betting risks more without strong equity. Opponent’s passivity suggests they may also be weak, but showdown is safer than bluffing.\nReason: Minimal showdown value and inconsistent with aggression; checking controls pot size and leverages position in decision-making.\nAnswer: 0'}
